<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buyid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Buyids, Buyid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buyid dynasty was a Shia Iranian dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> that controlled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the tenth and eleventh centuries. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 945, the Buyids ruled while keeping the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> as figureheads. They were displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril Beg</a> entered Baghdad in 1055, a key step in the rise of the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Buyid dynasty" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ro4444 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buyids_970.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military power increasingly devolved to provincial commanders and then to outright successor dynasties: the Tahirids, Saffarids and Samanids in the east, and later the <b>Buyids</b> and Seljuks at the centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west Mahmud defeated the Qarakhanids near Balkh in 1008, annexed Khwarazm in 1017 and took Rayy from the <b>Buyids</b> in 1029.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tughril entered Baghdad in 1055, freeing the Abbasid caliph Al-Qa'im from the tutelage of the Shia <b>Buyids</b> and receiving formal recognition as sultan, a title meaning power or authority, from the caliph himself, a legitimising act that transformed a steppe warlord into the temporal protector of Sunni Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 861 Turkish soldiers murdered or deposed four caliphs in nine years, and from 936 a military commander, the amir al-umara, held real power, until the <b>Buyids</b> took Baghdad in 945 and left the caliph a figurehead.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasid caliph al-Mustakfi, who had reportedly intrigued against Mu'izz al-Dawla with some of the <b>Buyids</b>' Daylamite officers, was deposed on Mu'izz al-Dawla's orders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-29">29 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu'l-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz, chief of the Kurdish Shadhanjan tribe, established himself at Hulwan on the road from Baghdad to Hamadan around 990, as <b>Buyid</b> authority weakened.</blockquote><a href="/state/annazid-dynasty">Annazid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
